{
  "raw": {
    "https://employment.unl.edu/postings/102869": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Professor - Psychometrics and Quantitative Methods",
      "description": "University of Nebraska-Lincoln | Assistant/Associate Professor - Psychometrics and Quantitative Methods Skip to Main Content University of Nebraska-Lincoln Employment Website Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Assistant/Associate Professor - Psychometrics and Quantitative Methods Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Click “Apply for this Job,” complete the Faculty/Academic Administrative Information Form, and attach the following documents: Letter of application. Current curriculum vitae. Contact information for three professional references. Three samples of scholarly writing. Combine into a single document and attach as “Other Document.\" In addition, arrange for three reference letters with contact information to be sent by the review date to Dr. James Bovaird, QQPM Search Committee Chair, either by mail (114 Teachers College Hall, University of Nebraska, Lincoln, NE 68588-0345) or email ( jbovaird2@unl.edu ) sent directly from the reference . Candidates wishing additional information are encouraged to contact Dr. Bovaird by phone (402-570-4000) or email ( jbovaird2@unl.edu ). Position Information Position Information EEO Statement The University of Nebraska does not discriminate based on race, color, ethnicity, national origin, sex, pregnancy, sexual orientation, gender identity, religion, disability, age, genetic information, veteran status, marital status, and/or political affiliation in its programs, activities, or employment. See: Notice of Nondiscrimination Working Title Assistant/Associate Professor - Psychometrics and Quantitative Methods Department Educational Psychology-1047 Requisition Number F_260108 Posting Open Date 10/15/2026 Application Review Date: (To ensure consideration, please submit all application materials before review date) 11/16/2026 Posting Close Date Open Until Filled Yes Description of Work The Department of Educational Psychology seeks applicants for a nine-month tenure-track Assistant or Associate Professor in our Quantitative, Qualitative, and Psychometric Methods ( QQPM ) program beginning August 2027. We are seeking individuals with a doctorate in psychometrics, measurement, quantitative methods, or a related field to be completed no later than August 2027. Expertise or interest in modern psychometrics (e.g., modern issues in testing, large language models, fairness, equating, computerized adaptive testing, diagnostic classification models, automatic item generation, scale/test development), latent variable modeling approaches (e.g., IRT , SEM , person-centered analysis, factor analysis), multilevel modeling, and/or a related approach is required. Experience or interest in methodological applications relevant to the areas of applied statistics, causal inference, non-parametric and/or categorical data, Bayesian estimation and/or inference, and/or program evaluation is desirable. RESPONSIBILITIES Developing and maintaining an on-going program of methodological research leading to publications in major quantitative, psychometric, statistical, and/or methodological journals. Collaborating with faculty across the College of Education and Human Sciences, UNL , and the University of Nebraska system on methodological issues related to substantive research projects. Effective instruction, student research advisement, and student mentoring. Participating in faculty governance. Participating in and/or preparing applications for external funding. Providing leadership and service at local, state, and national levels. ABOUT US The Department of Educational Psychology ( https://edpsyc.unl.edu ) is nationally known for educational research that combines theoretical depth with practical significance; systematic mentorship of students and junior faculty; active collaboration among faculty and students and commitment to creating an environment where all individuals can thrive. We are a graduate-oriented department that offers master’s and doctoral degree specializations in QQPM , Development and Learning Sciences, School Psychology, and Counseling Psychology. The Department houses the Buros Center for Testing, the Nebraska Evaluation and Research Center, and the Nebraska Internship Consortium in Professional Psychology. The Department is also closely affiliated with the Nebraska Center for Research on Children, Youth, Families, and Schools, an interdisciplinary center identified as an institutional Program of Excellence. The Department cooperates with four other academic departments in providing learning experiences for undergraduate and graduate students in the College of Education and Human Sciences. The College of Education and Human Sciences strives to be a vibrant, welcoming, and caring community driven by our mission to enhance the lives of individuals, families, schools, and communities, and strengthening the relationships among them. Residents of Lincoln, Nebraska enjoy a vibrant and growing community with a strong economy, low cost of living, and affordable housing ( http://www.lincoln.org ). Lincoln offers an abundance of parks, hiking and biking trails, and exceptional public schools. Notable entertainment and cultural opportunities can be found on and off campus in the downtown, Haymarket, and Railyard areas, and at the Lied Center for Performing Arts. Minimum Required Qualifications Doctorate in psychometrics, measurement, quantitative methods, or a related field conferred no later than August 2027. Research productivity and an emerging publication record. Potential for excellence in teaching and mentoring graduate and/or undergraduate students. Expertise or interest in modern psychometrics (e.g., modern issues in testing, large language models, fairness, equating, computerized adaptive testing, diagnostic classification models, automatic item generation, scale/test development), latent variable modeling approaches (e.g., IRT , SEM , person-centered analysis, factor analysis), multilevel modeling, and/or a related approach. Preferred Qualifications Record of scholarly publications commensurate to faculty rank. Evidence of success in pursuing and securing external funding. Experience or interest in methodological applications relevant to the areas of applied statistics, causal inference, non-parametric and/or categorical data, Bayesian estimation and/or inference, and/or program evaluation. Work Location (City, State) Lincoln, NE Pre-Placement Driving Record Review Required Criminal History Background Check Required Posted Salary How to Apply Click “Apply for this Job,” complete the Faculty/Academic Administrative Information Form, and attach the following documents: Letter of application. Current curriculum vitae. Contact information for three professional references. Three samples of scholarly writing. Combine into a single document and attach as “Other Document.\" In addition, arrange for three reference letters with contact information to be sent by the review date to Dr. James Bovaird, QQPM Search Committee Chair, either by mail (114 Teachers College Hall, University of Nebraska, Lincoln, NE 68588-0345) or email ( jbovaird2@unl.edu ) sent directly from the reference . Candidates wishing additional information are encouraged to contact Dr. Bovaird by phone (402-570-4000) or email ( jbovaird2@unl.edu ). For questions or accommodations related to this position contact Dr. James Bovaird, Search Committee Chair Email: jbovaird2@unl.edu Phone: 402-570-4000 Job Category Job Category (old) Faculty Tenure/Tenure Leading Job Type Full-Time Position funded by grant or other form of temporary funding? No If Temporary, indicate end date Planned Hire Date August 2027 Appointment End Date Organizational Location Default Location Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Required Documents Required Documents Letter of Interest Curriculum Vitae List of References Other Document Optional Documents Connect with #UNL UNLincoln on Facebook @UNLincoln on Twitter UNL on YouTube @unlincoln on Instagram University of Nebraska–Lincoln on LinkedIn unlincoln on Pinterest UNLincoln on Snapchat unlincoln on Spotify Campus Links Directory Employment Events Libraries Maps Office of the Chancellor Nebraska Today Policies Emergency Planning and Preparedness Institutional Equity and Compliance Notice of Nondiscrimination Privacy Policy Student Information Disclosures TIPS Incident Reporting University of Nebraska–Lincoln About the Big Ten Conference © 2017 University of Nebraska–Lincoln · 402-472-7211 University of Nebraska System To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://employment.unl.edu/postings/102848": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Professor - Inorganic/Materials Chemistry",
      "description": "University of Nebraska-Lincoln | Assistant/Associate Professor - Inorganic/Materials Chemistry Skip to Main Content University of Nebraska-Lincoln Employment Website Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Assistant/Associate Professor - Inorganic/Materials Chemistry Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Click \"Apply for this Job\" and complete the faculty information form. Attach the following documents: Cover letter. Curriculum vitae. Statement of research interests (up to six pages). Statement of teaching interests (up to one full page). Please combine the statements into a single document and upload as “Other Document.\" Applicants must also arrange for three confidential letters of reference to be sent by email to chemsearch@unl.edu or to: Search Committee Attn: Arianna Alonso Department of Chemistry University of Nebraska-Lincoln Lincoln, NE 68588-0304 Inquiries regarding the position or the application process should be directed to chemsearch@unl.edu or: Search Chair Department of Chemistry University of Nebraska-Lincoln 545 Hamilton Hall Lincoln, NE 68588-0304 (Fax: 402-472-9402) Position Information Position Information EEO Statement The University of Nebraska does not discriminate based on race, color, ethnicity, national origin, sex, pregnancy, sexual orientation, gender identity, religion, disability, age, genetic information, veteran status, marital status, and/or political affiliation in its programs, activities, or employment. See: Notice of Nondiscrimination Working Title Assistant/Associate Professor - Inorganic/Materials Chemistry Department Chemistry-1078 Requisition Number F_260096 Posting Open Date 10/15/2026 Application Review Date: (To ensure consideration, please submit all application materials before review date) 12/08/2026 Posting Close Date Open Until Filled Yes Description of Work The Department of Chemistry at the University of Nebraska–Lincoln ( UNL ) invites applications for a tenure-track position in inorganic chemistry at the Assistant or Associate Professor level, with an anticipated start date of August 2027. We seek a candidate whose research and teaching interests focus on the design and synthesis of materials that exhibit novel electronic and magnetic properties with potential applications in quantum technologies, spintronics, and nanoelectronics. Examples of relevant materials include but are not limited to: semiconductor nanostructures for single-photon emission, van der Waals heterostructures, topological materials, molecular spin chains, and chemically tailored qubits. It is anticipated that the successful candidate’s research will create opportunities for collaboration with researchers in physics as well as materials science and engineering. Applicants must demonstrate the capacity to teach undergraduate and graduate chemistry courses. UNL is a land grant institution with a strong commitment to research, teaching, outreach, and service. UNL is a Carnegie Foundation “Doctoral Universities: Highest Research Activity” university and is committed to further strengthening its research standing. Minimum Required Qualifications A Ph.D. in chemistry or an allied discipline. Preferred Qualifications Strong record of scholarly activity as evidenced by a significant publication record. Potential for developing an externally funded research program in inorganic chemistry. Expertise in the teaching of chemistry content or pedagogy to undergraduate and/or graduate students. Work Location (City, State) Lincoln, NE Pre-Placement Driving Record Review Required Criminal History Background Check Required No Posted Salary How to Apply Click “Apply for this Job” and complete the faculty information form. Attach the following documents: Cover letter. Curriculum vitae. Statement of research interests (up to six pages). Statement of teaching interests (up to one full page). Please combine the statements into a single document and upload as “Other Document.\" Applicants must also arrange for three confidential letters of reference to be sent by email to chemsearch@unl.edu or to: Search Committee Attn: Arianna Alonso Department of Chemistry University of Nebraska-Lincoln Lincoln, NE 68588-0304 Inquiries regarding the position or the application process should be directed to chemsearch@unl.edu or: Search Chair Department of Chemistry University of Nebraska-Lincoln 545 Hamilton Hall Lincoln, NE 68588-0304 (Fax: 402-472-9402) For questions or accommodations related to this position contact Brooke Crawley, badam2@unl.edu Job Category Job Category (old) Faculty Tenure/Tenure Leading Job Type 9 Month Position funded by grant or other form of temporary funding? No If Temporary, indicate end date Planned Hire Date August 2027 Appointment End Date Organizational Location Default Location Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Required Documents Required Documents Cover Letter Curriculum Vitae Other Document Optional Documents Connect with #UNL UNLincoln on Facebook @UNLincoln on Twitter UNL on YouTube @unlincoln on Instagram University of Nebraska–Lincoln on LinkedIn unlincoln on Pinterest UNLincoln on Snapchat unlincoln on Spotify Campus Links Directory Employment Events Libraries Maps Office of the Chancellor Nebraska Today Policies Emergency Planning and Preparedness Institutional Equity and Compliance Notice of Nondiscrimination Privacy Policy Student Information Disclosures TIPS Incident Reporting University of Nebraska–Lincoln About the Big Ten Conference © 2017 University of Nebraska–Lincoln · 402-472-7211 University of Nebraska System To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
